{
  "format": "sfxgen/1",
  "name": "Footsteps: gravel",
  "description": "A loop of eight steps in boots on a loose, damp gravel path, outdoors, no echo, here at a run. Each step: the heel digs in and a run of small stones grinds and clicks, thick at first and quickly thinning, on a dull, dead thump of the packed ground; the ball of the foot rolls forward with a longer, finer crunch and a little grit is thrown off the toe; under both, the gritty grind of the sole sinking in. Every crunch is a different handful of stones, left and right feet weigh differently, each step is a little bigger or smaller, earlier or later, and twice in the loop a stone is kicked away and hops to rest. At a run, as here, the steps close up, heel and toe land almost together, the crunch is shorter and harder, the ground thumps and gravel sprays behind each landing; Speed takes it back to a slow, careful walk where the toe rolls in well after the heel. The loop is exactly eight step intervals long and wraps, so it repeats without a seam.",
